<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Metz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Divodurum, Mettis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Metz is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where the Seille joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>. It began as Divodurum, the main town of the Mediomatrici, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was an important road junction later called Mettis after the people. It was a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdom</a> of Austrasia and a free imperial city, came under French rule in 1552, and was German from 1871 to 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/metz.jpg" alt="Metz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philippe Gisselbrecht/ Ville de Metz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metz_centre_ville.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
